nswer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a0b2308-5ec0-5f5e-9192-b34d743148a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "=+\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486793} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3fb587f-d8af-5cf0-9966-1bfb2fed1833" :answer-field/key "field-6" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487265} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4acf7e0f-2106-53cc-9639-b3207e1a41ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "=-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486756} {:answer/id #uuid "54e7b8de-fe38-5075-be29-cec30a3e2c88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "=+\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486778} {:answer/id #uuid "935532cf-dd16-513d-9732-e19cb9a54202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486917} {:answer/id #uuid "be190364-76b5-55c4-af60-92f63074f555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "935532cf-dd16-513d-9732-e19cb9a54202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486917} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3ec095c-9a68-5427-9d9b-5bd0d6b73ffe" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "60981b2b-035b-5158-a36c-3b0a5f268ae1" :question/math-academy-id "q-253560" :question/problem "Consider the set $B=(-1,\\infty ).$ Complete the following reasoning to determine $inf(B)$ and $sup(B).$\n\nThe infimum of $B$ is the {{field-1}} of $B,$ and the supremum is the {{field-2}} of $B.$\n\nSince $B$ is {{field-3}}, we have $inf(B)$ {{field-4}}.\n\nSince $B$ is {{field-5}}, we have $sup(B)$ {{field-6}}." :question/worked-solution "The infimum of $B$ is the greatest lower bound of $B,$ and the supremum is the smallest upper bound of $B.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\le -1$ is smaller than or equal to the elements of $B.$ Thus, the set $(-\\infty ,-1]$ contains only lower bounds of $B.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x>-1$ is **not** a lower bound of $B.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $B$ that is smaller than $x$ for any $x>-1.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-1]$ is the set of lower bounds of $B,$ and\n\n$$\ninf(B)=max(-\\infty ,-1]=-1.\n$$\n\nNext, to find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSince $B$ is unbounded above, $B$ has no upper bounds, and $sup(B)=+\\infty .$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of a set $B$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $B,$ and the supremum is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $B.$\n\nSince $B$ is $\\text{bounded below},$ we have $inf(B)=-1.$\n\nSince $B$ is $\\text{unbounded above},$ we have $sup(B)=+\\infty .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487399 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162157 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fee0e08c-5a7a-5f78-83aa-39679bb3b17b" :knowledge-point/title "Supremum and Infimum of an Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d6e4397-fca7-502a-957d-5547a9e1e822" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value 