m of this set.\n\nSince $B$ is unbounded below, $B$ has no lower bounds, and $inf(B)=-\\infty .$\n\nNext, to find the supremum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $B$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, we have the following:\n\n- Any number in the set $x\\ge \\pi $ is greater than or equal to the elements of $B.$ Thus, the set $[\\pi ,\\infty )$ contains only upper bounds of $B.$\n\n- Any number in the set $x<\\pi $ is **not** an upper bound of $B.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $B$ that is greater than $x$ for any $x<\\pi .$\n\nTherefore, $[\\pi ,\\infty )$ is the set of upper bounds of $B,$ and\n\n$$\nsup(B)=min\\,[\\pi ,\\infty )=\\pi .\n$$\n\nThe completed reasoning is given below.\n\nThe infimum of a set $B$ is the $\\text{greatest lower bound}$ of $B,$ and the supremum is the $\\text{smallest upper bound}$ of $B.$\n\nSince $B$ is $\\text{unbounded below},$ we have $inf(B)=-\\infty .$\n\nSince $B$ is $\\text{bounded above},$ we have $sup(B)=\\pi .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487344 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162158 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffe2b2c0-23a0-5bd0-8bc7-c12139820b61" :knowledge-point/title "Supremum and Infimum of a Sequence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21924e2c-8b26-5c46-847f-8a11b488aaf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "inf(A)=2\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486661} {:answer/id #uuid "96d1ac88-15ae-5c73-a141-867400b768e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "inf(A)=\\frac{5}{2}\\notin A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486929} {:answer/id #uuid "970f9583-5c69-59ca-ba57-45f24412729b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "inf(A)=\\frac{5}{2}\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486931} {:answer/id #uuid "b7cbdaa1-5a69-566a-b744-1cb7429950de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "inf(A)=2\\notin A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486996} {:answer/id #uuid "bb516a43-df07-5641-b235-fe98c46c7066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$inf(A)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7cbdaa1-5a69-566a-b744-1cb7429950de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "inf(A)=2\\notin A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486996} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6d681e8-6c35-50f8-ba73-9aaaae945560" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487258}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "79e51d03-1539-5ef1-be77-7756304d3608" :question/math-academy-id "q-253831" :question/problem "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{2+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=2+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/78/7879f99e6dd09e7ada4c1d920b78504df70bf0c511380ea21728a755d6590d23.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing and converges to $2.$ \n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number in the set $x\\le 2$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number in the set $x>2$ is **not** a lower bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find a sufficiently large $n$ so that $2+{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}$ is smaller than $x$ for any $x>2.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,2]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,2]=2.\n$$\n\nNotice that $2$ is *not* the minimum of $A$ (the set $A$ has no minimum). Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ninf(A)=2\\notin A.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487350 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162159 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4649783b-3436-55a5-b779-1ca3de17c535" :knowledge-point/title "Supremum and Infimum of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "903caf77-7916-5c64-b9e2-451a898585c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "903caf77-7916-5c64-b9e2-451a898585c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486913} :answer-field/id #uuid "e79c778e-75ba-5f9f-8b03-a34683fe89e0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487266}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "956f7419-f174-5327-980b-e8eb653a771d" :question/math-academy-id "q-253859" :question/problem "![](images/37/372aa80e15465694347dce5325537a10be1217e83050d609dfeec0347bad1116.png)\n\nWhat is the infimum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n${inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The infimum of a function is the infimum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=[-2,2].$\n\nTo find the infimum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\le -2$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y>-2$ is **not** a lower bound of $A$ because $-2,$ an element of $A,$ is smaller than $y.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-2]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)=inf(A)=max(-\\infty ,-2]=-2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162532 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162159 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4649783b-3436-55a5-b779-1ca3de17c535" :knowledge-point/title "Supremum and Infimum of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2757b05-4b56-5d3d-96d7-e2c4c400904c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160511} {:answer/id #uuid "81c15513-de6c-507a-a4e4-85a00ef2493e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486878} {:answer/id #uuid "8e411409-7d1f-5737-886f-eec8dd70737b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486907} {:answer/id #uuid "977220d8-a69f-5fe3-9d16-eebe5663a8a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486934} {:answer/id #uuid "a15a5743-365d-5cb6-8f7a-759bebb3adb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "=-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486962}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a15a5743-365d-5cb6-8f7a-759bebb3adb5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "=-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486962} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddbcf90c-4d50-5f2e-bdb5-4cbe53b866b2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186160509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3c7fc87-bd49-59f4-938b-555482882671" :question/math-academy-id "q-253861" :question/problem "![](images/db/db233bb76aeb4692d932967a8a61dd7ad675a7df7adb1a006568cf12d25d5511.png)\n\nWhat is the infimum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n$\\,{inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)$\n {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The infimum of a function is the infimum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=(-\\infty ,3).$\n\nTo find the infimum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nIn our case, $A$ has no lower bounds. We can always find an element $x$ such that $f(x)$ is smaller than $y$ for any $y.$ \n\nTherefore, ${inf}_{x\\in R}f(x)$ $=-\\infty .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487298 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162159 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4649783b-3436-55a5-b779-1ca3de17c535" :knowledge-point/title "Supremum and Infimum of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e00bd87d-5f08-5393-9463-9cdcd001373e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e00bd87d-5f08-5393-9463-9cdcd001373e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487081} :answer-field/id #uuid "4c08a640-f274-5c79-a1a1-0eb159327882" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487173}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1a350bae-823f-50ee-90ca-2add36d5d3d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-253896" :question/problem "![](images/0d/0d745d274250c84f6701ff22d17d0c6506a9f03f4a1a461e09f609a0ea11d99b.png)\n\nWhat is the supremum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n${sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a function is the supremum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=[-2,1).$\n\nTo find the supremum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $y\\ge 1$ is an upper bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $y<1$ is **not** an upper bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find an element of $A$ that is greater than $y$ for any $y<1.$\n\nTherefore, $[1,\\infty )$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and\n\n$$\n{sup}_{x\\in R}f(x)=sup(A)=min[1,\\infty )=1.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487332 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162159 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4649783b-3436-55a5-b779-1ca3de17c535" :knowledge-point/title "Supremum and Infimum of a Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "560fbf54-d06b-5360-a195-76877c86b33e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486782} {:answer/id #uuid "5df91ad0-b4ef-5be9-8cb0-c61b9391c511" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "=-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486804} {:answer/id #uuid "999b27c8-912c-51fc-a11a-66b74ddf77ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486941} {:answer/id #uuid "d577c117-bc3c-53e3-be7a-c468c23a624b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "=+\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d577c117-bc3c-53e3-be7a-c468c23a624b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "=+\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487061} :answer-field/id #uuid "22e3bb45-2a66-5b11-9cff-86ec4e98fa2f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487150}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5b171f8f-d26a-5943-99d9-8e4c4110d0e4" :question/math-academy-id "q-253899" :question/problem "![](images/56/567aa249f2ed84b537d20d4137a28e0df1cd3e4c942f4cf305bd864da32b8bac.png)\n\nWhat is the supremum of the function $y=f(x)$ shown above?\n\n$\\,{sup}_{x\\ge -1}f(x)$\n {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The supremum of a function is the supremum of the range of the function.\n\nIn this case, the range of $f$ is $A=[0,\\infty ).$\n\nTo find the supremum of a set $A,$ we must find the set containing **all** upper bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of upper bounds) and take the minimum of this set.\n\nIn our case, $A$ has no upper bounds. We can always find an element $x$ such that $f(x)$ is greater than $y$ for any $y.$ \n\nTherefore, ${sup}_{x\\ge -1}f(x)$ $=+\\infty .$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487366 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162158 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffe2b2c0-23a0-5bd0-8bc7-c12139820b61" :knowledge-point/title "Supremum and Infimum of a Sequence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fa639dc-5e64-5e81-95cb-48ba2a291f76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "inf(A)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486626} {:answer/id #uuid "314f5e4e-8552-5814-90d8-a38916281f19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "inf(A)=1\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486698} {:answer/id #uuid "39e361d5-1d4c-5a24-9857-936433e94187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "inf(A)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\notin A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486717} {:answer/id #uuid "3a52c1c9-d934-5a2b-a0a5-58f417b57457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$inf(A)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486718} {:answer/id #uuid "c27fc419-17a3-5364-b645-76f73a7ab6e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "inf(A)=1\\notin A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487023}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fa639dc-5e64-5e81-95cb-48ba2a291f76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "inf(A)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486626} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4df8d23-1851-528c-ba22-425776fee81a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487257}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aafdf357-e5ff-5cb5-9dea-31946c44ff01" :question/math-academy-id "q-253910" :question/problem "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/42/42f6ea3d1c14b0ccb8774e284dc6812486ace7eda512e9c1b94df27e09445e6e.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is an oscillating sequence converging to $0.$ The minimum of $A$ is \n\n$$\nmin(A)={a}_{2}=\\frac{(-1)^{2+1}}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number $x\\le {a}_{2}$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number $x>{a}_{2}$ cannot be a lower bound because ${a}_{2},$ an element of $A,$ is smaller than $x.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2}]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,-\\frac{1}{2}]=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that $-\\frac{1}{2}$ is the minimum of $A.$ Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ninf(A)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\in A.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487405 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162158 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ffe2b2c0-23a0-5bd0-8bc7-c12139820b61" :knowledge-point/title "Supremum and Infimum of a Sequence"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d7b7700-3e95-510c-a153-82350219ac3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$inf(A)$ does not exist" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486620} {:answer/id #uuid "65aa0c48-3279-5f91-ae4c-1449b2527192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "inf(A)=-1\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486818} {:answer/id #uuid "c1ad51fd-d8f5-59d7-8e16-ad0e1d154496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "inf(A)=-\\frac{3}{10}\\notin A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487020} {:answer/id #uuid "d4e0666f-2b5a-53c8-b905-436b08bc6583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "inf(A)=-1\\notin A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487059} {:answer/id #uuid "de845076-a6ce-55d2-9cd1-cde3a48b2d44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "inf(A)=-\\frac{3}{10}\\in A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4e0666f-2b5a-53c8-b905-436b08bc6583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "inf(A)=-1\\notin A" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487059} :answer-field/id #uuid "73153ef3-6520-50a9-82e3-ddb42b9894a2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487203}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd2b3866-cbeb-5f5a-bcbc-008f4c1cdcd4" :question/math-academy-id "q-253912" :question/problem "Consider the following set.\n\n$$\nA=\\{-1+{(\\frac{7}{10})}^{n}\\,:\\,n\\in N\\}\n$$\n\nFor this set, we have that {{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The infimum of $A$ is the greatest lower bound of $A.$\n\nTo find the infimum of this set, we must find the set containing **all** lower bounds of $A$ (i.e., the set of lower bounds) and take the maximum of this set.\n\nThe elements of $A$ are the terms of the sequence ${a}_{n},$ given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=-1+{(\\frac{7}{10})}^{n},\\,n\\in N=\\{1,2,3,4,…\\}.\n$$\n\nLet's draw some points of our sequence on the real line:\n\n![](images/8b/8be6d8824c8cbf1fb66d64d3ccc027f2949149adc9f95cf4d1f3a814baec80b3.png)\n\nWe see that ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing and converges to $-1.$ \n\nSo, in our case, we have that\n\n- any number in the set $x\\le -1$ is a lower bound of $A,$\n\n- any number in the set $x>-1$ is **not** a lower bound of $A.$ In other words, we can always find a sufficiently large $n$ so that $-1+{(\\frac{7}{10})}^{n}$ is smaller than $x$ for any $x>-1.$\n\nTherefore, $(-\\infty ,-1]$ is the set of lower bounds of $A,$ and \n\n$$\ninf(A)=max(-\\infty ,-1]=-1.\n$$\n\nNotice that $-1$ is *not* the minimum of $A$ (the set $A$ has no minimum). Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\ninf(A)=-1\\notin A.\n$$"}]}
